<p>
  I was in an interview and the recruiter, at the end, confirmed that an AI notetaker had been running the whole time. Then a second interviewer said the same thing. Then a third. All of them disclosed it after the fact, none of them before. This is chaos. This is a compliance nightmare.
</p>

<p>
  This is the current state of AI in hiring: tools deployed mid-experiment, legal still forming a view, candidates in the middle. Experiments are not exempt from the rules.
</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>The rules have changed in a way that reaches far beyond whether you use AI at all.</p>
</blockquote>

<h2>The wild west of AI adoption</h2>

<p>
  AI vendors rushed the market faster than procurement could evaluate them, faster than legal could form a stance, and faster than HR could understand what was actually running inside the tools they were already using. Add pressure from leadership to "start using AI" without a clear roadmap or system in place and chaos ensues straight to the courtroom.
</p>

<p>
  One-off tools aside, almost all companies use an applicant tracking system and most ATS's have AI embedded in their core functionality, not just as a feature you toggle on. Greenhouse, Workday, Lever, iCIMS have ranking, scoring, candidate summaries, and pipeline analytics. Even our emails write themselves. That is the AI.
</p>

<p>
  Nobody clicked "enable artificial intelligence." They clicked "send rejection" and "advance to the next stage." The AI was already inside those actions, logging, scoring, and learning from every decision made in the system.
</p>

<p>
  When a company says "we don't use AI in hiring," they are usually describing their intention, not their tech stack. And intention is not a legal defense.
</p>

<h2>What it's already costing</h2>

<p>This is not theoretical exposure. The cases are already in federal court.</p>

<p>
  Workday's AI hiring tools rejected a candidate's application more than 80 times, often within minutes of submitting the application. The candidate is African American, over 40, and disabled. In May 2025, a federal judge certified the case as a nationwide collective action. It is the first of its kind. Millions of applicants may be included.
</p>

<p>
  iTutor Group settled a case with the EEOC for $365,000 after programming its recruitment software to automatically reject female applicants over 55 and male applicants over 60. One candidate figured it out by submitting the same application twice with different birth dates. Only the younger one received an interview invitation.
</p>

<p>
  CVS settled in 2024 after HireVue's platform analyzed candidates' facial expressions during video interviews and assigned each person an "employability score" that measured, among other things, their "innate sense of integrity and honor." Massachusetts called it a lie detector test.
</p>

<p>
  Intuit refused an accommodation request for human generated captioning from a hearing impaired candidate citing their automated subtitles as sufficient. It was an internal candidate application for promotion that required an AI video interview. When the AI returned a low score and recommended the deaf candidate "practice active listening," the ACLU filed a complaint with the EEOC.
</p>

<p>
  Amazon scrapped a resume screening tool in 2018 that was trained on ten years of hiring data. The data was mostly male. The tool learned to penalize the word "women's," as in women's chess club, and downgraded graduates of women's colleges. AI didn't create bias, it surfaced a decade of biased decision making through pattern recognition.
</p>

<p>
  None of these outcomes required malicious intent. Companies simply deployed tools in the name of efficiency and accidentally shed light on a broken system. There is no reversing course. The only way out is through.
</p>

<p>The only way through is to address the broken system, not avoid AI.</p>

<h2>The new standard</h2>

<p>
  Hiring bias is not new. "Culture fit" has been legal cover for building teams full of the same person for decades. "Not the right vibe" has never required documentation. A rejected candidate had no recourse because there was no paper trail, no audit right, and no standard of proof.
</p>

<p>
  Bias was the default operating system, and nobody had to defend it because nobody could see it. That has changed. Not just for companies using AI, but for every company making hiring decisions.
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Colorado's AI Act (June, 2026)</strong> gives candidates the right to appeal an automated hiring decision and requires employers to explain how it was made.</li>
  <li><strong>Illinois (HB3773, Jan., 2026)</strong> requires employers to disclose which AI product was used, and what decisions it affects.</li>
  <li><strong>New York City (Local Law 144, 2023)</strong> requires annual independent bias audits to be publicly posted.</li>
  <li><strong>California (FEHA, Oct., 2025)</strong> holds employers accountable for discriminatory outcomes from automated systems regardless of whether a human made the final call.</li>
  <li><strong>EU AI Act (Aug, 2026)</strong> high-risk AI provisions add urgency for any company with global hiring operations. US headquarters does not opt you out of EU scope.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  The right to know, to appeal, to see the audit, do not stay neatly inside the boundary of "AI-assisted decisions." They create a documentation standard. And that standard applies to everything the system touches.
</p>

<h2>The reversal</h2>

<p>Candidates are now using AI to build cases, not just keyword resumes.</p>

<p>
  The same tools companies use to screen, candidates use to audit. A rejected candidate with ChatGPT and a subpoena right can now cross-reference their qualifications against the job description, check the public bias audit, identify who was hired, and map the timeline. That is not a hunch. That is a case file.
</p>

<p>
  The thing companies have always gotten away with — "culture fit," "gut feel," "we went in a different direction" — is now being made inside systems that log every action, timestamp every decision, and produce a record that survives discovery. A candidate who suspects bias in a human decision made inside an AI-governed system has the tools and the legislation to back them up.
</p>

<p>
  The power asymmetry just flipped. Companies spent a decade using AI to filter candidates faster than humans could. Candidates are now using AI to fight back faster than legal can respond.
</p>

<h2>Opting out does not opt you out</h2>

<p>
  The governance follows the liability. The liability exists wherever a candidate can show a pattern, a timestamp, a qualification gap, a demographic skew, with or without an algorithm doing the work.
</p>

<p>What compliance actually requires is not a policy document. It is operational infrastructure.</p>

<ul>
  <li>A pre-deployment impact assessment that tests the tool on your data before candidates go through it.</li>
  <li>Bias monitoring that runs on a schedule, not on the day someone files a complaint.</li>
  <li>A human reviewer with documented override authority, not theoretical authority.</li>
  <li>Candidate rights management that someone is actually responsible for.</li>
  <li>An audit trail that survives a subpoena.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  That infrastructure is a serious undertaking. It is also the new cost of hiring. Because the alternative is not the way things were. The alternative is that the way things were is now discoverable.
</p>

<p>
  The tools are imperfect, the science is unsettled, and the vendors are overselling confidence they haven't earned. The question was never whether AI deserves trust. The question is who is accountable when it's wrong.
</p>

<p>
  Right now, courts across the country are assigning accountability whether companies are ready or not.
</p>
